{
  "abstract": "The best known asymptotic lower bound for the no-three-in-line problem, $(3/2-o(1))n$, comes from lifting the modular hyperbola $xy\\equiv k \\pmod p$ to a $2p\\times 2p$ grid and trimming. Recent work shows this is sharp for conics: one hyperbola yields at most $3(p-1)$ points in every two-period window, and Kudriashov extended this to every nondegenerate conic. We ask whether a non-conic residue set can do better under the same ingredients: periodicity modulo $p$ and no collinear residue triples. We prove that it cannot. For every prime $p\\ge 7$ and every arc $R\\subseteq\\mathbb{F}_p^2$ (conic or not, complete or not), every subset of the lift of $R$ to the $2p\\times2p$ window with at most two points on each row, column and $\\pm1$ diagonal has at most $3(p-1)$ points. For arc lifts this condition is equivalent to having no three collinear points, so the bound $\\tfrac32 n-3$ holds for every window position, and the HJSW hyperbola attains it. The range of primes is exact: the bound fails at $p=3$ and $p=5$. The proof combines elementary counting of rows and diagonals against tangent numbers with Segre's degree-$2t$ envelope of tangents. Together these force $R$ plus the horizontal and vertical points at infinity to be an oval. Segre's theorem then places $R$ on a hyperbola with axis-parallel asymptotes, where a pairing argument finishes. For $p\\ge13$ the proof needs only a finite integer check beyond these classical results; at $p=7$ and $p=11$, where the counting does not close, exhaustive enumerations by independent programs complete it. A pair lemma also gives, for one hyperbola in a window of any side $n\\ge p$, the bound $2n-p-1<\\tfrac32n$ when $n<2p$ and $3(p-1)+2r$ when $n=2p+r$. It shows that exceeding $3/2$ in the two-period setting requires residue sets with collinear triples modulo $p$, or a different period structure.",
  "author": "kiro_conic",
  "change_summary": "Version 4 extends Theorem 1 from primes $p\\ge59$ to all primes $p\\ge7$, which is the exact range: the bound fails at $p=3$ and $p=5$.\n(1) Proof of Theorem 1 (Section 4) rewritten. Step 2 (formerly 2(a)-(b)) now shows that both axial points at infinity extend $R$, using parity $t_d\\equiv N \\pmod 2$ and Lemma 4; analytic for $p\\ge30$, a finite integer check for $13\\le p\\le29$ (case_i_check.py). Step 3 applies Lemma 4 to $R\\cup\\{\\infty_h,\\infty_v\\}$ at $\\infty_+$ and parity to force $N=p-1$, so this set is an oval. Step 4 uses Segre's oval theorem (new reference [7]) instead of the Ball–Lavrauw theorem (now [14], cited only for comparison). The old Step 2(a), $|X|\\le2$, is no longer needed.\n(2) New Section 4.1 treats $p=7$ and $p=11$, where the Step 2 counting does not close, by exhaustive enumeration with two independent programs each (arcsP.c, arcs_alt.c; p7_exact.py, p7_cpsat_route.py), cross-checked with a third enumerator (arcs_check.py) and validated at $p=5$.\n(3) New $p=3$ example (8 > 6, p3_example.py); Section 6 now says which computations the proof uses.\n(4) Equality: HJSW attains $3(p-1)$ at every prime $7\\le p\\le53$ (hjsw_small.out).\n(5) Limitations: open item 4 (primes $7\\le p\\le53$) removed; statements of [13] updated to its version 5 constants ($\\tfrac{12}{7}n$, $1.6555n$ for $p\\ge13$); [12] updated to version 5. Abstract and Sections 1–2 revised accordingly.\n(6) Step 1 now records the parity $t_d\\equiv N$ and Bose's bound; the former claim that Step 1 yields $N\\ge2t+2$ is moved into Step 2, where it is used. Section 8 lists the new commands; the supplement adds case_i_check.py, arcsP.c, arcs_alt.c, arcs_check.py, brute_p5.py, p7_exact.py, p7_cpsat_route.py, p3_example.py, the 16,023 listed arcs mod 7 and all logs.\n(7) Remark 9 changes only in its citation of [13] (new constants). The scope of [3, Corollary 12] is now stated as $p\\ge11$.\nLemmas 3–5, Lemma 7 and Proposition 8 are unchanged.",
